  "observations": [
    "Passive review returns only counts and a fingerprint bound to the selected client/config and state identities; it creates no archive or owner.",
    "Explicit acceptance retains current config bytes and all displaced config inodes. Existing pending ownership is adopted only when the typed current document and recorded ownership transition agree.",
    "Metadata copies are validated before exclusive manifest publication in a private archive group; original metadata is relocated only afterwards. Old config versions remain in their client directory.",
    "Stale config, another target, changed metadata and corrupted copies stop archive publication. Separate owned children exit before and after manifest publication; reopen/resume and normal Undo work.",
    "64 active records can be archived to allow another Apply/Undo. 64 completed archive groups stop further archives without deleting old groups or changing the current config.",
    "All three native adapter manager fixtures verify read, explicit archive, reopened ownership and typed Undo. The panel requires checkbox confirmation and blocks duplicate/pending actions; genuinely delayed old-scope responses are discarded."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Native fixtures use synthetic client metadata and ready endpoint objects. Installed client discovery, a real native picker/window, packaged lifecycle, and live provider traffic remain separate H048 acceptance.",
    "Only app-owned metadata is archived; old config files remain beside the client config. No automatic pruning or storage-space saving is claimed. Abandoned prepared groups also consume the bounded archive-group allowance.",
    "Archive and ownership use separate atomic renames and revalidation, not strict cross-file CAS or a physical power-loss guarantee. The archive manifest is structurally validated on reopening; full archive-copy rehash is done during publication, not on every later config operation.",
    "macOS 15.8.2 ARM64 evidence does not establish Intel macOS, Windows 11 or Linux support. Windows review explicitly reports this APFS-only feature as unsupported."
  ],
